 is ((01)(12))^3 = the identity, giving THREE fixed points and therefore THREE components. That is exactly what makes it the Borromean rings, and the corpus says so elsewhere \u2014 rem:meson-to-proton-via-borromean has B_3 'confining three quarks', and rem:linking-relations-table-table1-data gives p (+) n = 6 components, i.e. 3 per nucleon. Why it is load-bearing rather than a slip of wording: the very next sentence is the proof's engine, 'Knots carry half-integral spin; links carry integral spin.' Under that dictionary a 3-component nucleon has INTEGRAL spin, which contradicts the spin-1/2 nucleon the whole proposition rests on and would give every nucleus integral spin regardless of A. Either the dictionary or the identification has to change; the two cannot both stand. The same false 'single-component knot' appears in the proposition block. Note also that the dictionary itself is an external postulate \u2014 component count does not determine spin as a matter of topology \u2014 which contradicts rem:nuclear-spin-parity-novelty's claim that the framework reproduces spin-statistics 'without external postulates'.","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:l00","model":null,"at":"2026-08-22","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-nonspeakable","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"no trap of this class; full-context read","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:l00","model":null,"at":"2026-08-22","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-performed-warmth","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"no trap of this class; full-context read","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:l00","model":null,"at":"2026-08-22","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-rhetorical-pivot","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"pass on the trap criterion. SECOND MATH FINDING, not fixed inline: that 'However' sentence dismisses a contradiction rather than resolving it, and leaves the contradiction on the page. The section states w(beta) = C(A,2) mod 2 and then 'w even <=> bosonic <=> J in Z'. Since C(A,2) is even exactly when A = 0 or 1 mod 4 \u2014 which the block itself derives \u2014 that chain yields J in Z iff A = 0,1 mod 4, whereas the proposition says J in Z iff A is even. They disagree for every A = 1 or 2 mod 4, and the table one block over refutes the writhe version outright: 1H has A = 1 (C(1,2) = 0, even, so 'bosonic') yet J = 1/2, and 2H has A = 2 (C(2,2) = 1, odd, so 'fermionic') yet J = 1. Proposed: either drop the writhe-parity equivalences, or state the writhe criterion as a separate claim that is refuted by the data, with the (a)/(b) scope classification. Two lesser notes. (a) The Jones--Wenzl paragraph does no work as written: it says p_14 projects onto V_7 while 14N has J = 1, then asserts 'the nuclear link's embedding selects the J = 1 subrepresentation via the coupling pattern of 7 protons and 7 neutrons' with no mechanism \u2014 the projector gives the wrong answer and the correction is unspecified. (b) On the convention question, which I checked rather than assumed: delta = -(q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2}) is the STANDARD Temperley--Lieb loop parameter, and its use here is not a convention-C dependence. Its reciprocal is numerically z_C \u2014 at the proton pin 1/(q^{1/2}+q^{-1/2}) = 0.499320334184 and sqrt(q)/(q+1) = 0.499320334184, identical because the two expressions are algebraically equal \u2014 but delta appears here as the TL loop value, not as a Markov parameter, so the 2026-08-15 convention-D ruling does not bite on this block. Recording it because the numeral 0.4993 is otherwise the convention-C fingerprint.","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:l00","model":null,"at":"2026-08-22","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-superlative","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"no trap of this class; full-context read","reviewer":"agent","by":"agent:l00","model":null,"at":"2026-08-22","stale":false,"history":2},{"id":"trap-thesis-restatement","group":"Voice","result":"pass","severity":null,"notes":"no 